- `ADD_TEST OpticalConstructor.Tests.PlannerSymmetryTests`

**How to implement.** The point-group table of the Planner's layer 1, as pure Domain data (001 §M 'Layer 1, structural (exact, tabulated): the point group fixes the tensor components: isotropic 1, uniaxial 2, biaxial 3 + orientation; optical activity in 18 of the 21 non-centrosymmetric classes'; the S14.1 brief; specs/0046/.manual/018 §3 and its source 'Gyrotropic point groups (18 of 21)'). The optical class REUSES `Anisotropy` (`MaterialComplexityEditor.fs:Anisotropy`: `Isotropic | Uniaxial | Biaxial`, how many independent principal axes the eps carries), and a crystal record's class REUSES `CrystalParameters.anisotropy` (`LibraryFacets.fs`, step 355); no second class type is declared. Each doc is one to three lines citing spec 0050 S14.1; every case name has two words; no `bool` appears. (1) New `Berreman/OpticalConstructor/OpticalConstructor.Domain/PlannerSymmetry.fs`, `module PlannerSymmetry` in `namespace OpticalConstructor.Domain`, appended at the end of `OpticalConstructor.Domain.fsproj`'s compile list (one `<Compile Include="PlannerSymmetry.fs" />` line, no comment), because it reads `CrystalPhysics` (step 353), `LibraryFacets`, `MaterialComplexityEditor`, `MuellerInverse` and `Studies` (step 361). Module doc: the Measurement Planner's layer-1 tables, what a crystal's symmetry allows and what a look at a plate of it can see (spec 0050 S14.1). (2) `ComponentCount of int` with `.value`, a count of independent components or angles. (3) `type Anisotropy with member this.independentIndexCount : ComponentCount`: 1 for `Isotropic`, 2 for `Uniaxial`, 3 for `Biaxial`. (4) `Centrosymmetry` = `CentrosymmetricClass | NonCentrosymmetricClass` and `Gyrotropy` = `GyrotropicClass | NonGyrotropicClass` (whether the class's symmetry allows natural optical activity). Both are derived and never stored, so they carry no wire tag. (5) `PointGroup`, the 32 crystallographic point groups, one case each, named by its crystal class (Hermann-Mauguin symbol in brackets): `TriclinicPedial` (1), `TriclinicPinacoidal` (-1), `MonoclinicSphenoidal` (2), `MonoclinicDomatic` (m), `MonoclinicPrismatic` (2/m), `OrthorhombicDisphenoidal` (222), `OrthorhombicPyramidal` (mm2), `OrthorhombicDipyramidal` (mmm), `TetragonalPyramidal` (4), `TetragonalDisphenoidal` (-4), `TetragonalDipyramidal` (4/m), `TetragonalTrapezohedral` (422), `DitetragonalPyramidal` (4mm), `TetragonalScalenohedral` (-42m), `DitetragonalDipyramidal` (4/mmm), `TrigonalPyramidal` (3), `TrigonalRhombohedral` (-3), `TrigonalTrapezohedral` (32), `DitrigonalPyramidal` (3m), `DitrigonalScalenohedral` (-3m), `HexagonalPyramidal` (6), `TrigonalDipyramidal` (-6), `HexagonalDipyramidal` (6/m), `HexagonalTrapezohedral` (622), `DihexagonalPyramidal` (6mm), `DitrigonalDipyramidal` (-6m2), `DihexagonalDipyramidal` (6/mmm), `CubicTetartoidal` (23), `CubicDiploidal` (m-3), `CubicGyroidal` (432), `CubicHextetrahedral` (-43m) and `CubicHexoctahedral` (m-3m). Members, each a complete match with no wildcard arm: `this.symbol : string`, the bracketed symbol, a bar written as a leading minus; `this.anisotropy : Anisotropy`, the five cubic classes `Isotropic`, the nineteen tetragonal, trigonal and hexagonal ones `Uniaxial`, the eight orthorhombic, monoclinic and triclinic ones `Biaxial`; `this.centrosymmetry : Centrosymmetry`, `CentrosymmetricClass` for exactly the eleven Laue classes -1, 2/m, mmm, 4/m, 4/mmm, -3, -3m, 6/m, 6/mmm, m-3 and m-3m; `this.gyrotropy : Gyrotropy`, `GyrotropicClass` for exactly the eighteen 1, 2, m, 222, mm2, 4, -4, 422, 4mm, -42m, 3, 32, 3m, 6, 622, 6mm, 23 and 432, so the three non-centrosymmetric classes -6, -6m2 and -43m and every centrosymmetric class are `NonGyrotropicClass`; `this.orientationAngleCount : ComponentCount`, how many angles fix the principal axes that the lattice does not, 3 for the two triclinic classes, 1 for the three monoclinic ones and 0 for the rest; and `static member all : PointGroup list`, the 32 in the order listed. (6) `CrystalSymmetry = { anisotropy : Anisotropy; pointGroups : PointGroup list }`, what a crystal record's tensor form allows, with `member this.independentIndexCount : ComponentCount` (its anisotropy's) and `member this.orientationAngleCount : ComponentCount` (the largest of its point groups', 0 for none). (7) `type CrystalParameters with member this.symmetry : CrystalSymmetry`: `anisotropy` is `this.anisotropy` (step 355); `pointGroups` is, for `UniaxialCrystal`, the six classes whose gyration tensor is diag(g11, g11, g33), `TrigonalPyramidal`, `TrigonalTrapezohedral`, `TetragonalPyramidal`, `TetragonalTrapezohedral`, `HexagonalPyramidal` and `HexagonalTrapezohedral` (the record's own doc 'classes 3/32/4/422/6/622', `MuellerInverse.fs:178`, moved with the record to `CrystalPhysics.fs` by step 353), and for `TriclinicCrystal` and `AbsorbingTriclinicCrystal` the one class `TriclinicPedial` (class 1, their docs). No wire tag, store, conversion or other member is added: nothing stores a point group, and the Planner's prior reads a kit's crystal through its record (step 471). Tests: new `Berreman/OpticalConstructor/OpticalConstructor.Tests/PlannerSymmetryTests.fs`, `module PlannerSymmetryTests` in `namespace OpticalConstructor.Tests`, appended at the end of `OpticalConstructor.Tests.fsproj`'s compile list with no comment. `the 32 point groups split into 11 centrosymmetric and 21 non-centrosymmetric classes, 18 of them gyrotropic`: `PointGroup.all` holds 32 distinct symbols; the centrosymmetric ones are exactly the eleven above; the other 21 hold exactly 18 gyrotropic classes, and the three that are not are -6, -6m2 and -43m; no centrosymmetric class is gyrotropic. `a point group fixes how many independent principal indices and orientation angles a crystal has`: the isotropic classes are exactly 23, m-3, 432, -43m and m-3m, each with one independent index; the nineteen uniaxial classes have two and the eight biaxial ones three; the orientation angle count is 3 for 1 and -1, 1 for 2, m and 2/m, and 0 for the other 27. `a crystal record states its anisotropy and the point groups whose tensor form it holds`: over test-local records written as literals (a uniaxial crystal n_o 1.544, n_e 1.553, g11 3.0e-5, g33 -1.0e-5, step 355's probe; a triclinic one with distinct indices; an absorbing one with fifteen distinct values), the uniaxial crystal's symmetry is `Uniaxial` over the symbols 3, 32, 4, 422, 6 and 622, each gyrotropic, with two independent indices and no orientation angle; the triclinic and the absorbing crystal's is `Biaxial` over 1, with three indices and three orientation angles.

**Acceptance.** After the table lands, `PlannerSymmetry.PointGroup` MUST hold the 32 classes with 11 centrosymmetric, 18 gyrotropic (all non-centrosymmetric) and the 1 / 2 / 3 independent indices of isotropic / uniaxial / biaxial classes, and `CrystalParameters.symmetry` MUST state each record's anisotropy and point groups; constructor-unit-tests MUST pass, including `the 32 point groups split into 11 centrosymmetric and 21 non-centrosymmetric classes, 18 of them gyrotropic`, `a point group fixes how many independent principal indices and orientation angles a crystal has` and `a crystal record states its anisotropy and the point groups whose tensor form it holds`, the count rising by 3; and build, elevation and the twenty-one s45 invariants MUST pass.
